            <ArticleTitle>&quot;Beginning with the Smallest Intake&quot;: Children's Brain Development and the Role of Neuroscience in Global Environmental Health.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1242-1245</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0896-6273(17)30700-6</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.neuron.2017.08.009</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Early exposure to environmental toxins like lead, air pollution, and arsenic can have long-lasting and irreversible consequences for children's neurodevelopment, especially in the developing world. Though the number of pollutants increases each year, some neuroscientists are forging partnerships to improve measurement, raise awareness, and promote global health.</AbstractText>
